 used to refine important local regions.","key":"QotKVkaFjs"}],"key":"dtGyrzaV2r"},{"type":"thematicBreak","key":"jtiCdYVKt9"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary Conditions and Initial Conditions","key":"igx8KGfYeS"}],"identifier":"boundary-conditions-and-initial-conditions","label":"Boundary Conditions and Initial Conditions","html_id":"boundary-conditions-and-initial-conditions","implicit":true,"key":"Q4JRNMIJ1d"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary conditions are the known hydraulic conditions, such as flow depth, discharge, velocity, or pressure, that allow the discretized forms of the governing flow equations to be solved at each grid node. In FLOW-3D, upstream boundary conditions can be defined by specifying flow depth, velocity, discharge, or pressure, depending on the channel geometry and flow uniformity.","key":"Hsb8O0Nemx"}],"key":"ADIS8FiNXU"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the downstream boundary, a flow depth or pressure boundary condition is typically assigned to allow the model to compute backwater effects and maintain numerical stability, especially under subcritical flow conditions.","key":"Wxx9TswVUE"}],"key":"BAX0joxc6k"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary Conditions for the Containing Mesh Block","key":"I2j0e1T8mB"}],"identifier":"boundary-conditions-for-the-containing-mesh-block","label":"Boundary Conditions for the Containing Mesh Block","html_id":"boundary-conditions-for-the-containing-mesh-block","implicit":true,"key":"YO6Dva65UO"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"spread":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inflow:","key":"OwAWbN4aZJ"}],"key":"wzbrIki7bL"},{"type":"text","value":" Specify velocity components, volume flow rate, or pressure. If pressure is used, it is assigne